# /etc/rc -- the boot script, run by /etc/init as sh /etc/rc.
#
# THERE IS NO TERMINAL HERE.  runcom in cmd/init/init.c gives this script
# descriptors 0, 1 and 2 on the root DIRECTORY, which can be neither read
# nor written, so every line meant to be seen must redirect to /dev/console
# for itself.
#
# The hash is a real comment: this shell has one, which v7's had not -- see
# cmd/sh/README.md.  So this file no longer has to be written in `:' lines
# whose words are still parsed.

cat /etc/motd >/dev/console
date >/dev/console

# WHAT THE DATE SAYS, AND WHY IT IS THE SAME EVERY TIME.  This machine has no
# clock-calendar a program can read (include/sys/param.h), so main.c's iinit()
# seeds `time' from the root superblock's s_time -- and b6fsutil stamps that
# with -T ROOTTIME when it builds the image (kernel/test/CMakeLists.txt).  On a
# build image this line therefore prints `Sat Jul 25 08:23:0X GMT 2026', and
# the seconds are the only field the boot drift can move: everything left of
# them is a literal, and 08:23:00 is 37 seconds clear of a minute roll.
#
# WHICH TEST SEES IT is kernel/test/console and only that one.  /etc/rc does
# not run until the first shell has exited (cmd/init/init.c: shutdown, single,
# runcom, multiple), and console.ini is the one test that types the ^D that
# gets there; kernel/test/boot quits on the first prompt, with the disk still
# attached read-only.  console.ini's expect stops just short of the seconds.
#
# What the v7 rc did next still wants a program this system has not got yet:
# fsck and mount (task C4), cron and update (C10), accton (C8).  Each line
# comes back as its command is ported.  There is nothing to mount in any case
# -- one EC-5052 drive is the whole of the store, and swap lives on the drums.
#
# rm left that list with task C1b, and so did cp, ln and mv -- but the only
# line of the v7 rc that used rm cleared /tmp of an editor's leftovers, and
# nothing on this image writes there except the tests.  It comes back with
# ed (task C3), which is the first program that will.
#
# TWO MORE LINES OF THE v7 rc COULD RUN TODAY AND DELIBERATELY DO NOT.
# `cat /dev/null >/etc/utmp' would write the root filesystem on every boot,
# and nothing reads utmp until task C6 brings login, who and the rest of it.
# `PATH=/bin:/usr/bin' names a directory that does not exist here, and the
# shell's own default path already reaches /bin.
